# Lesson 26: Spotting Errors and Sentence Improvement

> Error-spotting rewards pattern recognition, not instinct: the same
> twenty traps recur in every paper, dressed in new sentences. This
> lesson collects those traps — agreement, tense, pronoun, preposition,
> and modifier faults — into rule-plus-example pairs and a solved table
> you can drill until each trap announces itself on sight.

## 1. How Error-Spotting Questions Work

The standard item prints a sentence split into three or four labelled
parts and asks which part contains the error; a fifth option says
*no error*. Sentence improvement gives you a sentence segment and four
alternative renderings, asking for the best one.

A three-pass method keeps you fast and calm:

1. **Read the whole sentence for sense** — many "errors" resolve once
   the intended meaning is clear.
2. **Check the verb chain first** — agreement, tense, and form errors
   live in the verb, and the verb is where half of all errors sit.
3. **Check the small words** — prepositions, pronouns, articles, and
   conjunctions carry the rest.

@diagram tree id="error-taxonomy" layout="tree" aria="Taxonomy of error types tested in competitive exams"
  "Common error families" -> "Subject-verb agreement"
  "Common error families" -> "Tense and verb form"
  "Common error families" -> "Pronoun use"
  "Common error families" -> "Preposition and collocation"
  "Common error families" -> "Modifiers and word order"
  "Common error families" -> "Redundancy and word choice"
@/diagram

[tip]
If two parts look suspicious, pick the one with the **verb**: verbs are
rule-governed and examiners know it. Doubt about a preposition is often
a doubt about a fixed collocation — memorise the partner, not the rule.
[/tip]

## 2. Subject-Verb Agreement Traps

The verb agrees with the true grammatical subject, not with the noun
that happens to sit nearest to it. Intervening phrases are the setter's
favourite camouflage.

| Trap | Wrong | Right |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Phrase between subject and verb | The quality of the mangoes were poor. | The quality of the mangoes **was** poor. |
| Each and every take a singular verb | Each of the boys were present. | Each of the boys **was** present. |
| Either ... or: verb agrees with the nearer subject | Neither the teacher nor the students was late. | Neither the teacher nor the students **were** late. |
| One of the + plural noun + singular verb | One of my friends are a doctor. | One of my friends **is** a doctor. |
| Collective noun as one unit | The committee were divided in its opinion. | The committee **was** divided in its opinion. |
| Amount treated as one sum | Ten kilometres are a long walk. | Ten kilometres **is** a long walk. |
| Pair nouns take a plural verb | My spectacles is broken. | My spectacles **are** broken. |
| A number of vs the number of | A number of students was absent. | A number of students **were** absent. |

[warning]
*A number of* means "several" and takes a plural verb, but *the number
of* is the subject "number" itself and takes a singular verb: *The
number of accidents has fallen.* Distinguish these by the article, not
by the following noun.
[/warning]

## 3. Tense and Verb-Form Traps

| Trap | Wrong | Right |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Since + point of time with perfect tense | He is living here since 2010. | He **has been living** here since 2010. |
| No sooner ... than (not when) | No sooner did he arrive when the bell rang. | No sooner did he arrive **than** the bell rang. |
| Hardly ... when | Hardly had he sat down than the phone rang. | Hardly had he sat down **when** the phone rang. |
| Sequence of tenses | He said that earth revolved round the sun. | He said that earth **revolves** round the sun (universal truth). |
| Double past markers | I did not went to school. | I **did not go** to school. |
| Conditional chain | If he will come, I will meet him. | If he **comes**, I will meet him. |
| Gerund after preposition | She is fond to dance. | She is fond of **dancing**. |
| Perfect infinitive after wish for the past | I wish I meet him yesterday. | I wish I **had met** him yesterday. |

[example]
Spot the error: *No sooner (a)/ the chief guest arrived (b)/ than the
anthem began (c)/ no error (d).*
*No sooner* demands an inverted auxiliary: *No sooner **did** the chief
guest arrive than ...* — the error is in part (b), which lacks the
auxiliary *did* before the subject.
[/example]

## 4. Pronoun Errors

| Trap | Wrong | Right |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Reflexive for emphasis after as, than, but | He is as tall as myself. | He is as tall as **I (am)**. |
| Its (possessive) vs their for a collective | The jury gave its verdicts separately. | The jury gave **their** verdicts separately (acting individually). |
| One ... one's | One should do their duty. | One should do **one's** duty. |
| Comparisons after than | She is taller than me in our class (formal writing). | She is taller than **I (am)**. |
| Who (subject) vs whom (object) | Whom do you think won the prize? | **Who** do you think won the prize? |
| Possessive with gerund | I was surprised at he going there. | I was surprised at **his** going there. |
| Order of pronouns (231 rule for faults) | I, you and he are at fault. | **You, he and I** are at fault. |

[important]
The famous **231 rule**: in a *confession* (taking blame) the order is
second person, third person, then first — *You, he and I will be
punished*; in a *credit* (good act) the order is 123 — *I, you and he
will be rewarded*. Only pronouns of different persons are reordered;
*you and I*, both subjects, simply keep *you* first.
[/important]

## 5. Preposition and Collocation Errors

These errors have no logic to rescue you; only the fixed pair helps.
The wrong partner *feels* right because a near-synonym takes a different
preposition.

| Wrong | Right | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| discussed about the matter | discussed the matter | discuss takes a direct object |
| comprises of five chapters | comprises five chapters | comprise = consist of |
| married with a doctor | married to a doctor | married pairs with to |
| inferior than | inferior to | Latin comparatives take to |
| prefer tea than coffee | prefer tea to coffee | prefer pairs with to |
| abides by the rules | abides by the rules (correct) | check the fixed partner, not instinct |
| desirous for | desirous of | desirous pairs with of |
| deprived from | deprived of | deprive pairs with of |
| ignorant about the law | ignorant of the law | ignorant pairs with of |
| congratulate him for his success | congratulate him on his success | congratulate pairs with on |

## 6. Modifier and Word-Order Errors

A **dangling modifier** opens a sentence describing an actor, and then
the sentence's subject turns out to be someone else entirely.

* Wrong: *Walking through the park, the flowers looked beautiful.*
  (the flowers were not walking)
* Right: *Walking through the park, **I found** the flowers beautiful.*

* Wrong: *He only died last week.* (reads as: he did nothing but die)
* Right: *He died **only** last week.* — place *only* immediately
  before what it limits.

| Misplaced element | Confusing sentence | Clear sentence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| only | She only sings on Sundays. | She sings on Sundays only. |
| almost | The child almost ate the whole cake. | The child ate almost the whole cake. |
| participial opener | Being a rainy day, we stayed home. | It being a rainy day, we stayed home. |
| relative clause | The dog bit the man with a leash. | The dog with a leash bit the man. |

## 7. Fifteen Solved Examples

| No. | Sentence with labelled parts | Error and correction |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | One of my (a)/ cousin (b)/ lives in Jaipur (c) | (b) cousins — one of the + plural noun |
| 2 | The Ganga (a)/ is one of (b)/ the holiest river of India (c) | (c) rivers — superlative needs plural |
| 3 | He as well as (a)/ his friends (b)/ were invited (c) | (c) was — as well as ignores the second noun |
| 4 | She has been (a)/ working here (b)/ since five years (c) | (c) for five years — duration takes for |
| 5 | I did not (a)/ go to the office (b)/ because I was ill (c) | no error — all parts correct |
| 6 | Each of the (a)/ answers were (b)/ checked twice (c) | (b) was — each takes a singular verb |
| 7 | The scenery (a)/ of Kashmir (b)/ are beautiful (c) | (c) is — scenery is uncountable |
| 8 | He is superior (a)/ than his colleague (b)/ in experience (c) | (b) to his colleague — superior takes to |
| 9 | Neither of the two (a)/ answers are (b)/ acceptable (c) | (b) is — neither of takes a singular verb |
| 10 | The teacher gave (a)/ us many advices (b)/ before the exam (c) | (b) advice — uncountable, no plural |
| 11 | If I (a)/ will see him, (b)/ I shall tell him (c) | (b) see him — no will in the if-clause |
| 12 | He is one of (a)/ the best batsman (b)/ in the team (c) | (b) batsmen — one of the + plural |
| 13 | I met (a)/ an European (b)/ at the fair (c) | (b) a European — European begins with a y-sound |
| 14 | She is looking (a)/ forward to meet (b)/ you soon (c) | (b) to meeting — to is a preposition here |
| 15 | The committee (a)/ have issued (b)/ its report (c) | (b) has issued — one body, singular verb |

Drill these pairs by covering the third column and reconstructing the
correction aloud; the pattern, not the sentence, is what you are
memorising.

